[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Advanced TypeScript: Conditional Types and the infer Keyword"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"TypeScript conditional types and the infer keyword are powerful tools for creating flexible, type-safe abstractions. They allow your types to make decisions based on other types, enabling advanced type transformations that mimic runtime logic."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Conditional Types"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A conditional type looks similar to the ternary operator in JavaScript. It describes a type relation test and selects one of two possible types depending on the outcome."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"type IsString<T> = T extends string ? "Yes" : "No";

type A = IsString<string>; // "Yes"
type B = IsString<number>; // "No""},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Distributive Conditional Types"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"When conditional types act on a generic type, they become distributive when the checked type is a naked type parameter. This means TypeScript will automatically apply the conditional type to each member of a union type."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"type ToArray<T> = T extends any ? T[] : never;

type NumericArray = ToArray<string | number>; // string[] | number[]"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"The infer Keyword"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"The infer keyword allows you to "unwrap" or "extract" type variables from within another type during the conditional type evaluation. You can think of it as declaring a new variable inside the condition."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"type ReturnType<T> = T extends (...args: any[]) => infer R ? R : any;

type Fn = () => string;
type Res = ReturnType<Fn>; // string"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Practical Example: Unwrapping Promises"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"A common use case for infer is unwrapping nested Promises to get the underlying resolved type."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"type UnwrapPromise<T> = T extends Promise<infer U> ? UnwrapPromise<U> : T;

type Complex = Promise<Promise<string>>;
type Resolved = UnwrapPromise<Complex>; // string"},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Conditional types bring programming-like logic directly into the type system."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Distribution happens automatically on naked generic type parameters."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use infer within the extends clause to capture and reuse internal structure types."}]
